 🗓️ On this day in 1859, a burst of charged particles from a massive solar eruption wreaked havoc on telegraph offices on both sides of the Atlantic. Known as the Carrington Event, the geomagnetic storm caused electric current to surge through telegraph wires, shocking operators and sparking fires. It even allowed operators on a Boston-to-Portland line to continue sending messages after both had unplugged their machines.

